WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.807
This query groups order rows by product.

00:00:02.807 --> 00:00:07.007
The output grain changes from one order row to one product.

00:00:07.007 --> 00:00:11.452
That grain change is important in PostgreSQL and in Spark.

00:00:11.452 --> 00:00:19.312
In Spark, GROUP BY can also create a shuffle because rows with the same product may need to be brought

00:00:19.312 --> 00:00:25.132
together across workers. Connect this idea to a lakehouse processing millions of web events across many files.

00:00:25.132 --> 00:00:30.557
Ask what could go wrong if the concept were misunderstood: could rows be duplicated,

00:00:30.557 --> 00:00:38.112
important records disappear, categories be interpreted incorrectly, or a report become difficult to reproduce?

00:00:38.112 --> 00:00:43.787
A strong analyst can explain both the SQL operation and the assumption behind it.

00:00:43.787 --> 00:00:49.200
After the example, summarize the idea in one plain-language sentence before moving on.
